<*
 Black-body white points of the colour temperature table, as Redshift computes them.

 One row per 100 K from 1000 K to 25100 K (242 rows, three channels each). Each row is the
 RGB multiplier of a black body at that temperature, normalised so that its largest channel is
 1.0 (red up to and including 6500 K, blue from 6600 K upward).

 Source: Redshift `src/colorramp.c`, `blackbody_color[]`
 (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jonls/redshift/master/src/colorramp.c, fetched; the rows were
 copied from that file by script). The table is credited there to Ingo Thies (2013), from the file README-colorramp.
 Cross-checked against wlsunset's `color.c` (kennylevinsen/wlsunset, the Planckian and
 daylight approximations, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kennylevinsen/wlsunset/master/color.c)
 at 2700 K, 6500 K and 10000 K: see `src/kelvin_test.c3` and `docs/research/color_impl.md`.

 The values are copied from the source by script, not retyped.
*>
